From: WinBoat
Subject: [PATCH] d3d10umd: video decoding and the video processor

The Direct3D 11 video DDI (RetrieveSubObject's video function table), so
programs get hardware decoding: players, browsers, ffmpeg's d3d11va, Media
Foundation. DXVA picture parameters and bitstream buffers become gallium
picture descriptions for virgl's video codec, which runs them on the host's
VA-API driver. H.264, HEVC Main and Main 10, VP9 profiles 0 and 2, AV1 Main
(8 and 10-bit) and MPEG-2 Main, as far as the host decodes them (the profiles
are listed from its caps; others are not offered and programs decode on the
CPU). Short slice formats only.

- NV12/P010 textures: each array slice a gallium video buffer whose planes are
  separate R8/R8G8 (R16/R16G16) textures: decoder output slices are the video
  codec's buffers (the host keeps its surface with each as the reference for
  later pictures), the others the texture's size. Views select a plane by
  format; maps, uploads and copies see the D3D layout (chroma rows after luma).
  Shader views of planes are kept on their plane (ShaderBindStage recreated
  views on a resource's first texture).
- Decoder buffers are system memory the program fills and SubmitBuffers reads.
  AV1 tiles and MPEG-2 slices are taken from their DXVA slice control buffers.
- The video processor: NV12/P010 or RGB to RGB with scaling, BT.601/709 and
  both ranges, through gallium's video compositor, on a pipe context of its own
  (flushed in order with the device's, waiting on each other's fences: Windows
  schedules each context by itself), with 2D-array views of the planes, as
  its shaders sample.
- CreateResource's NV12 and decoder buffer path still ends the device's
  allocation bookkeeping for the resource.
- VIOGPU_VIDEO_LOG=<file> logs what the video entry points refuse.
---
